<h2>What enrollment checks</h2>
<ul class="enom-list space-y-1">
    <li>The native service is active, yearly, has one unit, no coupon and no cancellation.</li>
    <li>The domain is active, has a confirmed future expiry and is a .com.</li>
    <li>No request for the domain is awaiting payment, queued, running, waiting for the registrar, uncertain or held.</li>
    <li>A complete registrar check from the last 24 hours. Its identity and expiry match the record, the registrar reports the domain as registered and paid, and registrar auto-renew is off.</li>
    <li>Registrar reads are allowed, and the saved Enom account matches the account that made the check.</li>
</ul>
<p>The domain page shows the first check that fails and the next step.</p>
<h2>What enrollment changes</h2>
<p>The domain moves to managed billing. Its renewal invoices then follow the registrar expiry, and the native service due date is cleared so stock billing does not invoice it. Enrollment records the registrar's domain ID and the account it belongs to.</p>
<p>Enrollment cannot be undone. Enroll and set renewal invoices to On in the same save. To stop invoices later, set renewal invoices to Off.</p>
<h2>Zero retail price</h2>
<p>When the retail price is zero, the same setting is called the renewal policy, because no customer invoice is issued. Enroll with the renewal policy On. Setting it to Off later stops new sponsored renewal requests for the domain.</p>
<p>The issue-day setting becomes the review start day. The due-day offset and credit choice are saved, but apply only if the retail price becomes positive. Any spending still needs a sponsorship approval. See Sponsorship help.</p>
<h2>New orders</h2>
<p>When Use registrar expiry for new domains is on, new registrations and transfers start in managed billing, using the server's issue and due days.</p>
